<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Cavares</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 121 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gallic people of the lower Rhône whose towns became Avignon, Cavaillon and Orange.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> names the Cavares as the leading people between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isere-river">Isère</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. Their towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avennio</a> (Avignon), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavaillon">Cabellio</a> (Cavaillon) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-orange">Orange</a>), and they had close commercial ties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes them as already largely Romanised in his day. Little is known of their earlier rulers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>They came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after 121 BCE. In 105 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a> destroyed two Roman armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> in their land, one of Rome's worst defeats. Arausio was refounded as a colony for veterans of the Second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, with its famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and arch.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cavares.jpg" alt="Cavares" width="320" height="144"><figcaption>Image: cgb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denier_au_buste_de_cheval_frapp%C3%A9_par_les_Cavares.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 121 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cavari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-210&amp;focus=cavares" aria-label="Cavares on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4745 554 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cavares lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5065 810h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5065 810h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day France. <a href="/atlas?year=-210&amp;focus=cavares">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

